---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const siteUrl = 'https://shadowharness.github.io/';
const pageUrl = `${siteUrl}en/security/`;
const title = 'Data Handling & Security — ShadowHarness';
const description =
	'How Shadow Hub protects Sidera Memory data, what Shadow sends and stores, and where each AI CLI remains separate.';
---

<SiteLayout
	lang="en"
	title={title}
	description={description}
	pageUrl={pageUrl}
	alternateJa={`${siteUrl}security/`}
	alternateEn={pageUrl}
>
	<main id="main-content" class="guide-page" tabindex="-1">
		<header class="guide-hero">
			<p class="eyebrow">DATA HANDLING &amp; SECURITY</p>
			<h1>Data Handling &amp; Security</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				This page covers what Shadow controls: Sidera Memory data transfer and storage,
				and the protections built into Shadow Hub.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="guide-layout">
			<aside class="guide-nav">
				<p>On this page</p>
				<nav aria-label="Data handling contents">
					<a href="#security">Shadow Hub security</a>
					<a href="#data">Data Shadow handles</a>
					<a href="#providers">AI CLI boundary</a>
				</nav>
			</aside>

			<article class="guide-content prose">
				<section id="security">
					<p class="eyebrow">01 / SERVER SECURITY</p>
					<h2>Shadow Hub security</h2>
					<div class="trust-grid">
						<div>
							<h3>HTTPS in transit</h3>
							<p>Connections between your PC and Shadow Hub are encrypted with HTTPS.</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>Separated by account</h3>
							<p>
								Memory data is separated into a container, data area, and internal network
								for each user. Containers run as non-root with a read-only root filesystem.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>Authenticated per device</h3>
							<p>
								Log upload and memory retrieval require a device token. The server stores
								only its SHA-256 hash, not the token itself, and tokens can be revoked per device.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div>
							<h3>Limited public surface</h3>
							<p>
								The memory API is limited to log upload, memory retrieval, and evidence
								retrieval. Administrative APIs are not public, and credentials are omitted
								from access logs.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section id="data">
					<p class="eyebrow">02 / DATA</p>
					<h2>Data Shadow handles</h2>
					<dl class="facts-list">
						<div>
							<dt>Your PC</dt>
							<dd>
								Each CLI keeps its raw log in its own storage location. Raw logs include
								input, AI responses, and command output.
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>Shadow Hub</dt>
							<dd>
								When Sidera Memory is enabled, newly appended log ranges are sent to the Hub.
								The Hub keeps the complete accumulated raw logs and the memory and indexes
								built from them.
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>The working AI pane</dt>
							<dd>
								Memory relevant to the current input is supplied as context for the task.
							</dd>
						</div>
					</dl>
					<p>
						An LLM is used for part of memory structuring. Structuring input is processed
						under settings that do not allow it to be used for model training.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section id="providers">
					<p class="eyebrow">03 / AI CLI</p>
					<h2>AI CLI boundary</h2>
					<p>
						ShadowHarness does not proxy AI CLI traffic or send providers an additional copy
						of your conversations. Codex, Claude Code, Grok, Gemini, and other CLIs connect
						to their services through the same path they use when run on their own.
					</p>
				</section>
			</article>
		</div>
	</main>
</SiteLayout>
